design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:291:**Case procedure: soldering order of the five case wires W1 to W4 and W7, the depth gauge step, the cell leads W5 and W6 and the thermistor TH1 (review 2026-11-13, minor m3; review 2, notes N3 and fault F5).** Every step marked "(lead)" is the lead's instruction, to be confirmed at the first build; the other values come from the file named. In steps 1, 4, 9 and 10, PM means design/mfg/assembly/process.md (version of 2026-11-13) and each new sentence names its section or its file and line; "my reading" and [CHOICE] mark what no file states.
design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:293:1. Build the case main board (change C5 of design/mfg/assembly/process.md, here PM). Do this before anything else. Nothing is powered at this step: no cell, no USB cable, no meter in voltage mode (PM C5: "nothing is powered at this step"). (a) Stencil: stainless steel, laser cut, 0.10 mm thick, cut from fab/case_main/gerber/case_main-F_Paste.gbr, 265 openings, smallest area ratio 0.764 (PM P2 table, row "Case main board, top (F)"; PM line 92 for why 0.10 and not 0.125 mm). The bottom face has no opening and gets no stencil (PM P2 table, row "Case main board, bottom (B)"). (b) Paste: Kester NP505-HR, SAC305, Type 4, no-clean (PM P1.2), kept in a refrigerator at 0 to 10 C (PM P1.2, storage). Print the top face only with a squeegee of 80 to 90 durometer (PM P6). Hand printing: align the stencil by its openings on the lands under the 10 times magnifier (PM P7, last bullet). (c) Placing: put every part of the top face where fab/case_main/case_main_position.csv says (rows with Side "top"; export_fab.sh, Part e of this file), with the values of fab/case_main_parts.csv. The 16 test pads TP1 to TP16 are on the bottom face and are copper only (Round 2, "Length used"): nothing is placed there. The USB socket J1 goes on with the other parts. The module U1 has moisture class 3 (E1 p.47): keep it in its sealed bag until it is placed (my reading, the same rule PM P3 gives for U3 of the ring). (d) Reflow once, with the profile of PM P3: ramp to 150 C at less than 2.5 C/s; soak 150 to 200 C in 90 to 110 s, then 200 to 217 C; 60 to 75 s above 217 C; at most 35 s above 230 C; peak 240 C, accepted 235 to 245 C on a thermocouple taped beside the most massive part; cool at 6 C/s or slower, and do not move the board while the solder is liquid. The radio module U1 accepts ONE reflow only (E1 p.47: "Solder the module in a single reflow."; PM P3 table, row "Case main board" U1: margin 0 passes, AT THE LIMIT). So this board goes through the oven once, and never again: a part that needs rework is put right by hand (see (e)), never by a second pass. (e) Inspect every joint at 10 times (PM P6, row Magnifier). Accept a part only when it sits in place and flat, and no solder bridges two neighbouring pads (the accept rule PM gives, from design/mfg/assembly/assembly.md line 99: "no solder bridge between neighbouring contacts", parts "in place and not tilted"); look hardest at the 0.5 mm pitch of the charger U2 (Round 1, Part c, "inside the 0.5 mm pitch of U2"). A joint that fails is put right by hand with the iron setting of PM P4 (340 C, 50 W, at most 7 s; my reading, PM gives no rework setting for these parts). The full written rule for a good reflowed joint is not in PM (PM C14: "a standard the lead names"): open, for the lead. The joints under the module U1 and under the thermal pad of U2 cannot be seen at 10 times, and no file gives a check for them: open, for the lead. (f) Then, by hand, the four shell slots of the USB socket J1 (the four plated slots, all with pad name SH, Part a of this file): Kester 275 wire 0.5 mm, iron 340 C, 50 W, at most 7 s per joint (PM C5; PM P4 table, rows "Case cell leads W5, W6" and "Case thermistor TH1 leads", the setting of the same kind of joint), the tip face no wider than the pad (PM P4, general rule). Inspect these joints at 10 times as in (e). The residue stays on the board; it is never washed (PM P1.5).
design/ecad/case/pcb/board-notes.md:297:5. Depth gauge, after the stakes are formed and before the main board ends are soldered (ring out of the case, wires hanging; source design/case/case-architecture.md line 219, "Builder's step (depth gauge, 0.01 mm digital, ring out, holder staked)"): with a 0.01 mm digital depth gauge measure how far each free plunger tip stands in front of the pin holder's front face, along the pin. The values are P2 and P3 1.170 mm, accepted within +-0.04 mm; P1 and P4 1.021 mm, accepted within +-0.03 mm (case-architecture.md line 219, from r4/pin_gauge.log, which I did not re-derive). A tip that stands out too little means the island sits too far back (pins too high): put stainless shim behind that island, between the island and the lug, in steps of 0.025 mm up to the shortfall (shim stock 0.025, 0.05 and 0.10 mm). A tip that stands out too much: lap the lug's face for that island on 600-grit paper on a flat plate by the excess. Then measure again. The source does not say how the holder is taken off for a shim or a lap; line 238 of the same file says holder and PCB2 come off only by cutting the stake heads, so a correction means new stakes (my reading, to confirm at the first build).
